  "version": 1,
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "title": "Q1",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "Explain the architectural tradeoffs of using a synchronous threading.Lock() versus using an asyncio.Lock().",
        "zh-cn": "说明使用同步 threading.Lock() 与使用 asyncio.Lock() 在架构上的权衡。"
      },
      "answer": "A threading.Lock protects shared state across real OS threads. When a thread can't get the lock, the whole thread just waits. That's fine here because the work inside the lock is tiny: a few list operations and some math, with nothing that waits on the network. It also works from any code, whether sync, threaded or async. So check() stays a normal function, which matches how server.py calls it.\n\nThe downside shows up in async servers like aiohttp. If a thread blocks on the lock, the event loop freezes with it and every other request waits too. With this limiter that pause is microseconds and usually has no contention, so it doesn't matter. It would matter if the locked section got slow, and you can never hold a threading.Lock across an await.\n\nAn asyncio.Lock only coordinates coroutines on one event loop. Waiting on it hands control back to the loop, so other requests keep running. But it isn't thread safe, so it does nothing if calls come from real threads, which the README says to expect. It also forces check() to become async, which would break the current server contract.\n\nSo I'd use threading.Lock when the critical section is fast, CPU only and might be hit from threads. I'd switch to asyncio.Lock only if I needed to await something while holding the lock, like a Redis call, and everything ran on one event loop."
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "title": "Q2",
      "prompt": {
        "en": "Explain how this architectural design would need to evolve for a multi-instance, horizontally scaled deployment (e.g., migrating from in-memory state to a distributed cache like Redis or Memcached).",
        "zh-cn": "说明在多实例、水平扩展部署中，这种架构设计需要如何演进（例如从内存状态迁移到 Redis 或 Memcached 这样的分布式缓存）。"
      },
      "answer": "Right now each server keeps its own state in memory. With 3 instances behind a load balancer, a client could get 3 times their limit, because each instance only sees part of their traffic. Restarts also wipe everyone's counters.\n\nThe simple fix is sticky routing: hash each client to one instance so its state lives in one place. It's cheap, but load gets uneven, and limits reset whenever you add or remove instances. It's fine as a stopgap, not a real answer.\n\nThe real fix is moving state into a shared store like Redis. Here's what changes:\n\nThe lock moves into Redis. Two instances can't share a Python lock, so each check runs as one Lua script. Redis runs scripts one at a time, so the expire, decide and update step is still atomic per key.\nEach policy gets a Redis structure.\nPolicy A becomes a sorted set per key. The script removes old timestamps with ZREMRANGEBYSCORE, counts with ZCARD, and adds the new one with ZADD if allowed.\nPolicy B becomes a hash per key holding the token count and last refill time. The same refill math runs inside the script.\nOne clock. Use Redis TIME instead of each server's clock, so skew between machines can't mess up windows or refills.\nTTLs replace cleanup. Set an expiry on each key so idle clients disappear on their own.\nStats move too. Keep the counters in Redis with INCR so /stats shows totals across all instances, not just one box.\nCheck becomes async. Every check is now a network call, so with aiohttp I'd use an async Redis client. That's the point where asyncio concerns actually start to matter.\n\nTradeoffs to plan for:\n\nLatency. Every request now pays a Redis round trip. If that's too slow, each instance can lease a small batch of tokens from Redis and hand them out locally. You give up a bit of accuracy for far fewer calls.\nFailure mode. If Redis goes down, you have to pick. Fail open and keep serving, risking overuse of LLM capacity. Or fail closed and block everything, which is an outage. I'd fail open with a strict local limiter as backup.\nRedis Cluster. Keys get spread across shards. That works fine as long as each script only touches one key, which this design does.\n\nAs for Memcached: it only has simple counters and compare and swap, with no scripting. It can do a basic fixed window counter, but not a sliding log or a clean token bucket without races. Redis is the better fit for both policies."
    }
  ]
}
